netron3d
========

netron3d is a fork of Netron (https://github.com/lutzroeder/netron) by Lutz
Roeder, distributed under the MIT License. Netron's copyright and licence are
retained in full in the LICENSE file; all additions here are made under the same
MIT terms.

Netron reads model files (ONNX, TensorFlow, PyTorch, TFLite, and more) and lays
their computation graph out in 2D. netron3d adds one thing Netron does not have:
a family-aware 3D renderer. For an architecture it recognises, it draws a
hand-laid 3D scene sized from the model's own numbers; for anything else it
leaves Netron's 2D graph untouched, which is the correct rendering for a general
graph.

Relationship to llm-viz
-----------------------

netron3d is inspired by llm-viz (https://bbycroft.net/llm, by Brendan Bycroft),
which renders a transformer beautifully in 3D — but only its own preset demo
networks, taking no model as input. llm-viz carries no licence, so no part of it
is copied or derived here. netron3d is an independent implementation of the idea
"show a real model in 3D", built on Netron's parsing rather than on llm-viz.
